While an assistive technology consumes the accessibility tree,
Ladybird keeps each element’s final name and description between
builds of the tree. It drops them on any change to a node the element
contains. It drops a labelable element’s on a change that touches a
label, a labelable element, or an id or for attribute, and those of
the elements with aria-labelledby or aria-describedby on a change
inside any element whose ID they reference. The other engines track
which elements depend on which more precisely. WebKit keeps a computed
AXProperty::AccessibilityText in each AXIsolatedObject, and updates
it when handleTextChanged() walks the changed node’s ancestors and the
objects labelForObjects() returns. Blink doesn’t keep a final name in
the renderer, but serializes only the AXObjects marked dirty, which
AXRelationCache::UpdateRelatedText() finds through the ancestors and
its reverse maps of aria-labelledby and aria-describedby targets.
Gecko caches each RemoteAccessible’s direct name in the parent
process, and computes a name from content or relations there when
asked; EventQueue::PushNameOrDescriptionChange() finds the dependents
through mDependentIDsHashes.
